public inherited sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {

    /**
     * Normalizes the Phone field of the given contacts so that only digits
     * and an optional leading '+' remain (e.g. '+44 (20) 7946-0018' becomes
     * '+442079460018').
     *
     * All contacts are saved in a single partial-success DML
     * (Database.update(contacts, false)), so a record that fails validation
     * does not block the others.
     *
     * @return map from the Id of each contact that could not be saved to its
     *         first error message; empty when all contacts were saved.
     *         Contacts without a Phone value are skipped entirely.
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> errors = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return errors;
        }

        // Query only the fields we need; Id is included implicitly.
        List<Contact> contacts = [
            SELECT Id, Phone
            FROM Contact
            WHERE Id IN :contactIds
        ];

        // Keep the original list order and collect only dirty records.
        List<Contact> toUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Contact c : contacts) {
            if (String.isBlank(c.Phone)) {
                // No phone: leave the contact out.
                continue;
            }

            String cleaned = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
            if (cleaned != c.Phone) {
                c.Phone = cleaned;
                toUpdate.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (!toUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            // partialSuccess = true: a failed record does not block the rest.
            List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.update(toUpdate, false);

            for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
                Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
                if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                    Database.Error firstError = sr.getErrors()[0];
                    errors.put(toUpdate[i].Id, firstError.getMessage());
                }
            }
        }

        return errors;
    }
}
```

Note on one small change I made to your described approach: I skip contacts whose cleaned phone equals the original value, so we only send genuinely changed records through DML. This avoids unnecessary save processing, avoids tripping no-op validation/save logic, and keeps the results list aligned with exactly the records we updated. If you truly want every contact with a phone re-saved even when unchanged, remove that guard — the `toUpdate[i].Id` alignment in the results loop still works either way.

Everything else follows your spec: a single `Database.update(contacts, false)` after the loop (bulk-safe for 200 records, well within governor limits), a loop over the save results, and a return map of Id → first error message.

